b/ref 1072 #uuid "12dc9838-1006-5e99-98b8-8a2304014d14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531560 #edb/ref 1074 "This is a two-step random process: first, a room of difficulty level $n$ is chosen randomly and uniformly from $1$ to $120,$ and then a puzzle piece with a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ We are asked to find the expected value of the puzzle piece selected in the second step.\n\n- Let $Y$ be the room difficulty level, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $120.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the puzzle piece number, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,120\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{120+1}{2}=60.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (60.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 61.5 \\\\  & =30.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531595 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531595 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531595 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531595 #edb/ref 1071 "A robot starts at the bottom of a staircase with $n$ steps, where $n$ is an integer chosen uniformly at random from $1$ to $10.$ The robot then climbs a uniformly random number of steps between $1$ and $n.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of steps the robot climbs?\n\nThe expected number of steps the robot climbs is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531595 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5923cf06-a8dd-57e8-81ff-5f4a640b9a2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531595 #edb/ref 1074 "This is a two-step random process: first, an integer $n$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ through $10,$ and then a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ through $n.$\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number: the number of steps in the staircase. Since an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $10,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,10\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the number of steps the robot climbs, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,10\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{10+1}{2}=5.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (5.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6.5 \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531605 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531605 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531605 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531605 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $p,$ $q,$ and $r$ with probabilities $0.5,$ $0.3,$ and $0.2,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]=8,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]=14,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]=10.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531605 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d436b5a-22ca-50fe-bbca-e3c952622b7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531605 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y=p$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]=8$\n\n- $Y=q$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]=14$\n\n- $Y=r$ with probability $0.2,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]=10$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=p]\\cdot P(Y=p)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=q]\\cdot P(Y=q) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=r]\\cdot P(Y=r) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.5+14\\cdot 0.3+10\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =4+4.2+2 \\\\  & =10.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531616 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275442" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531616 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531616 #edb/ref 1071 "An order picker in a warehouse is assigned to either Section $1$ or Section $2$ on any given day. With probability $0.65,$ the order picker works in Section $1,$ where the expected number of orders picked per day is $120.$ With probability $0.35,$ the order picker works in Section $2,$ where the expected number of orders picked per day is $95.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of orders picked per day?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87aec959-895e-5be8-8c69-b3c4045c5829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531616 #edb/ref 1074 "We are asked to compute the expected number of orders picked per day. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the section to which the picker is assigned.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of orders picked, and let $Y$ be the warehouse section to which the picker is assigned.\n\n- $Y=1$ with probability $0.65,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=120.$\n\n- $Y=2$ with probability $0.35,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]=95.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=2]\\cdot P(Y=2) \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.65+95\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =78+33.25 \\\\  & =111.25\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531624 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531624 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275544" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531624 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531624 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=2Y-2.$ If $\\text{E}[X]=8,$ what is $\\text{E}[Y]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a2b9353-2f5e-55ce-b33e-819639da3c0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531624 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and the value of $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation, which gives\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[2Y-2].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[2Y-2] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $\\text{E}[X]=8,$ so we solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-2 \\\\ 10 & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531640 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531640 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531640 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531640 #edb/ref 1071 "A salesperson visits one of three regions: North, South, or East. The probabilities that they are sent North, South, and East are $0.25,$ $0.5,$ and $0.25$ respectively.\n\nIf the expected number of sales made daily is $9$ in the North, $14$ in the South, and $11$ in the East, what is the expected number of sales made in a day?\n\nThe expected number of sales made is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531640 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cdbfe9c8-c3f2-5a6b-9ce7-99052b0abd68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531640 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the probability of being sent to one of three regions and the expected number of sales made. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which tells us how to compute the overall expected value by conditioning on which region the salesperson visits.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sales made in a day, and let $Y$ be the region the salesperson visited.\n\n- $Y=\\text{North}$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{North}]=9.$\n\n- $Y=\\text{South}$ with probability $0.5,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{South}]=14.$\n\n- $Y=\\text{East}$ with probability $0.25,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{East}]=11.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{North}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{North})+\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{South}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{South}) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{East}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{East}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 0.25+14\\cdot 0.5+11\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.25+7+2.75 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531642 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531642 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275580" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531642 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531642 #edb/ref 1071 "A robotic arm randomly selects a box in a warehouse containing $n$ items from $1$ to $150,$ with each value equally likely. The arm then randomly picks one of these $n$ items and scans its position number (from $1$ to $n,$ each value equally likely).\n\nWhat is the expected position number scanned?\n\nThe expected position number scanned is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531642 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d15a80b9-3b38-5de0-953c-45a827aaaf2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531642 #edb/ref 1074 "This is a two-step random process: first, a box is selected containing $n$ items uniformly from $1$ through $150,$ and then an item is chosen uniformly in one of the positions numbered $1$ through $n.$\n\n- Let $Y$ be the number of items in the box. Since this is an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $150,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,150\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the position number scanned, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,150\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{150+1}{2}=75.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (75.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 76.5 \\\\  & =38.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531643 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531643 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531643 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531643 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $-1,$ $0,$ and $1$ with probabilities $0.15,$ $0.55,$ and $0.3,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]=4,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]=9,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=8.\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531643 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3d68253-5058-5406-8e0e-23e4aaba826a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531643 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the conditional expectations of $X$ given different values of another random variable $Y,$ along with the probabilities of those values. This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes three values:\n\n- $Y=-1$ with probability $0.15,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]=4$\n\n- $Y=0$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]=9$\n\n- $Y=1$ with probability $0.3,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]=8$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=-1]\\cdot P(Y=-1)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=0]\\cdot P(Y=0) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=1]\\cdot P(Y=1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.15+9\\cdot 0.55+8\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.6+4.95+2.4 \\\\  & =7.95\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531649 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531649 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531649 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531649 #edb/ref 1071 "A tutoring agency assigns a tutor to either High School $A$ or High School $B.$ With probability $0.45,$ the tutor is sent to High School $A,$ where the expected number of students tutored in a day is $4.8.$ With probability $0.55,$ the tutor is sent to High School $B,$ where the expected number of students tutored is $6.2.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of students tutored in a day?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531649 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0fafa34-8718-5c91-847f-90668c3f437e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531649 #edb/ref 1074 "We are asked to compute the expected number of students tutored per day. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the high school the tutor is assigned to.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of students tutored in a day, and let $Y$ be the school to which the tutor is assigned.\n\n- $Y=A$ with probability $0.45,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=A]=4.8.$\n\n- $Y=B$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=B]=6.2.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=A]\\cdot P(Y=A)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=B]\\cdot P(Y=B) \\\\  & =4.8\\cdot 0.45+6.2\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =2.16+3.41 \\\\  & =5.57\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531651 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531651 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531651 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531651 #edb/ref 1071 "A ride-share driver starts every day in either the City Center or the Suburban Area. The driver starts the day in the City Center with probability $0.55,$ where the expected number of rides given per day is $14.3.$ The driver starts the day in the Suburban Area with probability $0.45,$ where the expected number of rides is $11.7.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of rides given in a day?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531651 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e734511f-0834-5433-966a-4ae3851cf360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531651 #edb/ref 1074 "We are asked to compute the expected number of rides given in a day. This is a classic application of the law of total expectation, which allows us to calculate an overall expected value by conditioning on a related variable — in this case, the driver's starting location.\n\nLet $X$ be the number of rides completed in a day, and $Y$ be the location from which the driver started.\n\n- $Y=\\text{City}$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{City}]=14.3.$\n\n- $Y=\\text{Suburban}$ with probability $0.45,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{Suburban}]=11.7.$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{City}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{City}) \\\\  & \\,+\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=\\text{Suburban}]\\cdot P(Y=\\text{Suburban}) \\\\  & =14.3\\cdot 0.55+11.7\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =7.865+5.265 \\\\  & =13.13\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531653 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531653 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531653 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531653 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=4Y-3.$ If $\\text{E}[Y]=10,$ what is $\\text{E}[X]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531653 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eff3dc19-9675-582f-bb2d-ae75355788e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531653 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and we are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[4Y-3]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[4Y-3] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[3] \\\\  & =4\\cdot 10-3 \\\\  & =37\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531658 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531658 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275424" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531658 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531658 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, where $Y$ takes values $a$ and $b$ with probabilities $0.55$ and $0.45,$ respectively. You are given the following conditional expectations:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=6,\\,\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\text{E}[X]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531658 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb48c25d-7d3e-5c68-83ac-d6034fcc4a05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531658 #edb/ref 1074 "We are asked to compute the expected value of $X,$ given conditional expectations of $X$ based on the value of another random variable $Y.$ This is a direct application of the law of total expectation, which states\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{y}\\text{E}[X∣Y=y]\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nIn this problem, $Y$ takes two values:\n\n- $Y=a$ with probability $0.55,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]=6$\n\n- $Y=b$ with probability $0.45,$ and $\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]=4$\n\nSo, we calculate our expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[X∣Y=a]\\cdot P(Y=a)+\\text{E}[X∣Y=b]\\cdot P(Y=b) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.55+4\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =3.3+1.8 \\\\  & =5.1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531661 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531661 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275538" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531661 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531661 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1.$ If $\\text{E}[Y]=\\sqrt{3},$ what is $\\text{E}[X]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531661 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fde0d020-b26d-5edb-a043-de5b1f35d4a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531661 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and we are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y+1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
